From: MeowArchMobile
Subject: [PATCH] file_sync_client.cpp: terminate the root-dir walk on Windows

copy_local_dir_remote() walks up from the destination to the filesystem root
to collect the directories it has to create:

    for (std::string path = rpath; !is_root_dir(path); path = Dirname(path))

and upstream's is_root_dir() returns true only when the path is all forward
slashes. On POSIX dirname() bottoms out at "/" so that is fine, but mingw's
dirname() returns a single BACKSLASH for the root of a POSIX-style path —
measured with the distro toolchain:

    dirname("/tmp")       -> "\"
    dirname("C:/x")       -> "C:\"
    dirname("Z:/tmp/w")   -> "Z:/tmp"
    dirname("\")          -> "\"        <- fixed point

So is_root_dir("\\") is false, the loop never terminates, directory_list grows
until the allocation fails, and every directory push on Windows dies with

    terminate called after throwing an instance of 'std::bad_alloc'

while single-file pushes work, because they never reach this loop.

Two changes, deliberately independent:

  * is_root_dir() treats either separator as root. Backslash is a separator on
    Windows (adb_is_separator() already says so), so this is not a Windows
    exception — it is the check matching the platform's path syntax.

  * the loop also stops when dirname() returns its own input. mingw has two
    such shapes ("C:\" and "//x", which it maps to "//x\"), and either one
    would spin forever on its own. Without the first fix this alone would be
    enough to stop the crash, but it would still push a bogus "\" entry into
    directory_list and then try to mkdir it.

POSIX behaviour is unchanged: "/" is all forward slashes, so it still exits on
the first test, and dirname("/") returns "/" so the fixed-point check would
stop the same loop one iteration earlier at worst — which it cannot reach,
because is_root_dir("/") is already true.

--- a/client/file_sync_client.cpp
+++ b/client/file_sync_client.cpp
@@ -1566,9 +1566,18 @@
 }
 
 // dirname("//foo") returns "//", so we can't do the obvious `path == "/"`.
+//
+// A separator of EITHER kind counts as root. Upstream only looked at '/' and got
+// away with it on POSIX, where dirname() bottoms out at "/". mingw's dirname()
+// instead returns a single BACKSLASH for the root of a POSIX-style path
+// (measured: dirname("/tmp") -> "\", dirname("C:/x") -> "C:\", and
+// dirname("\") -> "\"), so this test never became true and the walk below
+// appended "\" to directory_list forever, ending in std::bad_alloc. That made
+// every directory push on Windows die, while single-file pushes worked because
+// they never reach this loop.
 static bool is_root_dir(std::string_view path) {
     for (char c : path) {
-        if (c != '/') {
+        if (c != '/' && c != '\\') {
             return false;
         }
     }
@@ -1588,8 +1597,15 @@
     std::vector<copyinfo> file_list;
     std::vector<std::string> directory_list;
 
-    for (std::string path = rpath; !is_root_dir(path); path = android::base::Dirname(path)) {
+    // The fixed-point check is belt-and-braces on top of is_root_dir(): a
+    // platform whose dirname() returns its own input for some path shape
+    // ("C:\", or "//x" which mingw maps to "//x\") would otherwise loop
+    // forever, and the failure mode is a bad_alloc rather than an error message.
+    for (std::string path = rpath; !is_root_dir(path);) {
         directory_list.push_back(path);
+        std::string parent = android::base::Dirname(path);
+        if (parent == path) break;
+        path = std::move(parent);
     }
     std::reverse(directory_list.begin(), directory_list.end());
 
